.TH JSON_PARSE_STRALLOC 3 "JSON Utility" "LIBJSON" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
json_parse_stralloc - Parse a JSON string into a stralloc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <lib/json/json.h>
.PP
.B int json_parse_stralloc(charbuf* b, stralloc* out, int quoted);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B json_parse_stralloc
function parses a JSON string from the specified
.IR charbuf
and stores the result in the specified
.IR stralloc
structure.
If
.IR quoted
is non-zero, the function expects the string to be quoted with double quotes.
If
.IR quoted
is zero, the function expects the string to be unquoted and separated from the next token by a colon.
The function returns 1 on success, and 0 on failure.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I b
A pointer to a
.B charbuf
structure containing the JSON string to parse.
.TP
.I out
A pointer to a
.B stralloc
structure to store the result.
.TP
.I quoted
An integer indicating whether the string is quoted (non-zero) or unquoted (zero).
.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 on success, and 0 on failure.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following example demonstrates how to use the
.B json_parse_stralloc
function:
.PP
.B #include <lib/json/json.h>
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int main() {
.B \tcharbuf b = CHARBUF_INIT;
.B \tcharbuf_append(&b, "\"Hello, World!\"");
.B \tstralloc sa;
.B \tstralloc_init(&sa);
.B \tint ret = json_parse_stralloc(&b, &sa, 1);
.B \tif (ret) {
.B \t\tprintf("JSON string parsed successfully\n");
.B \t\tprintf("Parsed string: %s\n", sa.s);
.B \t\tstralloc_free(&sa);
.B \t}
.B \tcharbuf_free(&b);
.B \treturn 0;
.B }
.SH SEE ALSO
.B json_init,
.B json_free